+# Proactive demo: your data + synthetic data
+
+Shows Wisp's proactive features in the real Mac app. Your own Calendar, Reminders, Mail and Messages stay
+exactly as they are; synthetic items are added **only inside Wisp's own store**, are hidden outside demo
+mode, and `clear` removes them. Nothing is written to Apple Calendar, Reminders, Mail, Messages or Notes.
+
+## What the audience sees
+
+| Moment | What happens |
+| --- | --- |
+| Synthetic schedule appears beside yours | Today view and the notch chip: a meeting in ~45 min, a reminder due in ~3 min, an exam in 2 days, assignments |
+| Lead-time reminders | Native notifications at T-30m / T-10m, no model involved |
+| Daily brief | Deterministic summary; human mail separated from automated senders |
+| **Caught for you** (new) | `drop` delivers "Mom: Meet me in the Quad at 6PM". It is not on your calendar, so Wisp adds it, notifies you, and says what it added. Heads-ups follow at 30 and 10 minutes before |
+| What it stays quiet about | `drop --preset promo`, `scam`, `known`: promotions, scam shapes and things already on file produce no alert |
+
+## One-time setup on your Mac (about 15 minutes)
+
+The new code is backend code, so the app must be rebuilt once.
+
+```sh
+cd ~/Desktop/MOE_Project            # your checkout
+git apply /path/to/wisp-demo.patch  # the patch that came with this folder
+python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install -r requirements-runtime.txt   # skip if .venv exists
+./scripts/wisp-build all --allow-dirty   # builds a candidate under dist/; installs nothing
+```
+
+`--allow-dirty` is required because the patch is uncommitted. The build does not touch `/Applications`; run the
+candidate straight from `dist/` (below). macOS may ask for Calendar, Contacts, Automation or Full Disk Access
+again for a rebuilt app: grant them once, **before** the demo.
+
+## Before each demo
+
+```sh
+PY=.venv/bin/python
+$PY demo/wisp_demo.py preflight     # lists your REAL items for the next 48 h that will be on screen
+# quit Wisp from the menu bar, then:
+$PY demo/wisp_demo.py setup         # resets and adds the synthetic schedule (run while Wisp is quit)
+$PY demo/wisp_demo.py launch --app dist/<candidate>/Wisp.app
+```
+
+Leave that Terminal window open. Wait ~1 minute for the first sync (the brief and alerts hold back until
+Messages has synced). Then, on cue:
+
+```sh
+$PY demo/wisp_demo.py drop                   # the "Quad at 6PM" text (2 h ahead); alert within ~30 s
+$PY demo/wisp_demo.py drop --preset promo    # silent
+$PY demo/wisp_demo.py drop --preset scam     # silent
+$PY demo/wisp_demo.py drop --preset known    # silent: already on the calendar
+$PY demo/wisp_demo.py drop --minutes 300     # push the stated time further out
+$PY demo/wisp_demo.py status
+```
+
+`drop` prints what it expects ("ALERT" or "silent, and why") before the app reacts.
+
+## After
+
+```sh
+# quit Wisp, then
+$PY demo/wisp_demo.py clear      # removes every synthetic row and the overlay; your data is untouched
+```
+
+Open Wisp normally afterwards (no flags): it behaves exactly as before.
+
+## Know before you present
+
+- **Your real data is on screen.** `preflight` shows the calendar/reminder items. Mail and Messages appear in the
+  brief and chat too. Rehearse with screen sharing off; for a fully synthetic run, export
+  `WISP_HOME=$HOME/.wisp-demo` before `setup` and `launch` (a separate, empty Wisp; permissions and models
+  still come from your Mac).
+- **Real texts can trigger it too.** In demo mode, a real incoming text that states a time within 24 h and is
+  not on your calendar will also raise an alert and add a Wisp commitment (max 3 alerts a day).
+- **Rules are strict on purpose.** It needs an explicit am/pm (or a 24-hour time), only "today / tonight /
+  tomorrow" or no day, a plan word (meet, dinner, class...), and no question or hedge. Anything ambiguous is silent.
+- **Wisp's schedule, not Apple Reminders.** The alert says "Added to Wisp's schedule" because that is what
+  happened. Nothing is mirrored into the Reminders app.
+- **Chat needs oMLX and a model** running as usual. The proactive features above do not.
+- **A rebuilt app has not been run by the developer of this change** (it was written on a Linux machine).
+  Do a full rehearsal, and record a screen capture as a fallback.
